@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1456 = private unnamed_addr constant [27 x i8] c"call-with-shell-equals-true", align 1
@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1457 = private unnamed_addr constant [978 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for method calls that set the `shell` parameter to `true` or another\0Atruthy value when invoking a subprocess.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ASetting the `shell` parameter to `true` or another truthy value when\0Ainvoking a subprocess can introduce security vulnerabilities, as it allows\0Ashell metacharacters and whitespace to be passed to child processes,\0Apotentially leading to shell injection attacks.\0A\0AIt is recommended to avoid using `shell=True` unless absolutely necessary\0Aand, when used, to ensure that all inputs are properly sanitized and quoted\0Ato prevent such vulnerabilities.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AProne to false positives as it is triggered on any function call with a\0A`shell=True` parameter.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport subprocess\0A\0Auser_input = input(\22Enter a command: \22)\0Asubprocess.run(user_input, shell=True)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Security Considerations](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html#security-considerations)\0A", align 1
