@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1541 = private unnamed_addr constant [776 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for direct instantiation of `logging.Logger`, as opposed to using\0A`logging.getLogger()`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe [Logger Objects] documentation states that:\0A\0A> Note that Loggers should NEVER be instantiated directly, but always\0A> through the module-level function `logging.getLogger(name)`.\0A\0AIf a logger is directly instantiated, it won't be added to the logger\0Atree, and will bypass all configuration. Messages logged to it will\0Aonly be sent to the \22handler of last resort\22, skipping any filtering\0Aor formatting.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Alogger = logging.Logger(__name__)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Alogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\0A```\0A\0A[Logger Objects]: https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html#logger-objects\0A", align 1
