@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1544 = private unnamed_addr constant [1018 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `print` statements.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`print` statements used for debugging should be omitted from production\0Acode. They can lead the accidental inclusion of sensitive information in\0Alogs, and are not configurable by clients, unlike `logging` statements.\0A\0A`print` statements used to produce output as a part of a command-line\0Ainterface program are not typically a problem.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef sum_less_than_four(a, b):\0A    print(f\22Calling sum_less_than_four\22)\0A    return a + b < 4\0A```\0A\0AThe automatic fix will remove the print statement entirely:\0A\0A```python\0Adef sum_less_than_four(a, b):\0A    return a + b < 4\0A```\0A\0ATo keep the line for logging purposes, instead use something like:\0A\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Alogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\0A\0A\0Adef sum_less_than_four(a, b):\0A    logging.debug(\22Calling sum_less_than_four\22)\0A    return a + b < 4\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it will remove `print` statements\0Athat are used beyond debugging purposes.\0A", align 1
