@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1552 = private unnamed_addr constant [1401 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `__eq__` and `__ne__` implementations that use `typing.Any` as\0Athe type annotation for their second parameter.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe Python documentation recommends the use of `object` to \22indicate that a\0Avalue could be any type in a typesafe manner\22. `Any`, on the other hand,\0Ashould be seen as an \22escape hatch when you need to mix dynamically and\0Astatically typed code\22. Since using `Any` allows you to write highly unsafe\0Acode, you should generally only use `Any` when the semantics of your code\0Awould otherwise be inexpressible to the type checker.\0A\0AThe expectation in Python is that a comparison of two arbitrary objects\0Ausing `==` or `!=` should never raise an exception. This contract can be\0Afully expressed in the type system and does not involve requesting unsound\0Abehaviour from a type checker. As such, `object` is a more appropriate\0Aannotation than `Any` for the second parameter of the methods implementing\0Athese comparison operators -- `__eq__` and `__ne__`.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```pyi\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def __eq__(self, obj: typing.Any) -> bool: ...\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```pyi\0Aclass Foo:\0A    def __eq__(self, obj: object) -> bool: ...\0A```\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: The `Any` type](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#the-any-type)\0A- [Mypy documentation: Any vs. object](https://mypy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/dynamic_typing.html#any-vs-object)\0A", align 1
