@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1689 = private unnamed_addr constant [18 x i8] c"numpy2-deprecation", align 1
@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1690 = private unnamed_addr constant [1210 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of NumPy functions and constants that were removed from\0Athe main namespace in NumPy 2.0.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ANumPy 2.0 includes an overhaul of NumPy's Python API, intended to remove\0Aredundant aliases and routines, and establish unambiguous mechanisms for\0Aaccessing constants, dtypes, and functions.\0A\0AAs part of this overhaul, a variety of deprecated NumPy functions and\0Aconstants were removed from the main namespace.\0A\0AThe majority of these functions and constants can be automatically replaced\0Aby other members of the NumPy API or by equivalents from the Python\0Astandard library. With the exception of renaming `numpy.byte_bounds` to\0A`numpy.lib.array_utils.byte_bounds`, all such replacements are backwards\0Acompatible with earlier versions of NumPy.\0A\0AThis rule flags all uses of removed members, along with automatic fixes for\0Aany backwards-compatible replacements.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport numpy as np\0A\0Aarr1 = [np.Infinity, np.NaN, np.nan, np.PINF, np.inf]\0Aarr2 = [np.float_(1.5), np.float64(5.1)]\0Anp.round_(arr2)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport numpy as np\0A\0Aarr1 = [np.inf, np.nan, np.nan, np.inf, np.inf]\0Aarr2 = [np.float64(1.5), np.float64(5.1)]\0Anp.round(arr2)\0A```\0A", align 1
