@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1706 = private unnamed_addr constant [18 x i8] c"invalid-class-name", align 1
@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1707 = private unnamed_addr constant [861 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for class names that do not follow the `CamelCase` convention.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 8] recommends the use of the `CapWords` (or `CamelCase`) convention\0Afor class names:\0A\0A> Class names should normally use the `CapWords` convention.\0A>\0A> The naming convention for functions may be used instead in cases where the interface is\0A> documented and used primarily as a callable.\0A>\0A> Note that there is a separate convention for builtin names: most builtin names are single\0A> words (or two words run together), with the `CapWords` convention used only for exception\0A> names and builtin constants.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass my_class:\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass MyClass:\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.pep8-naming.ignore-names`\0A- `lint.pep8-naming.extend-ignore-names`\0A\0A[PEP 8]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/#class-names\0A", align 1
