@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1713 = private unnamed_addr constant [19 x i8] c"invalid-module-name", align 1
@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1714 = private unnamed_addr constant [1081 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for module names that do not follow the `snake_case` naming\0Aconvention or are otherwise invalid.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 8] recommends the use of the `snake_case` naming convention for\0Amodule names:\0A\0A> Modules should have short, all-lowercase names. Underscores can be used in the\0A> module name if it improves readability. Python packages should also have short,\0A> all-lowercase names, although the use of underscores is discouraged.\0A>\0A> When an extension module written in C or C++ has an accompanying Python module that\0A> provides a higher level (e.g. more object-oriented) interface, the C/C++ module has\0A> a leading underscore (e.g. `_socket`).\0A\0AFurther, in order for Python modules to be importable, they must be valid\0Aidentifiers. As such, they cannot start with a digit, or collide with hard\0Akeywords, like `import` or `class`.\0A\0A## Example\0A- Instead of `example-module-name` or `example module name`, use `example_module_name`.\0A- Instead of `ExampleModule`, use `example_module`.\0A\0A[PEP 8]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/#package-and-module-names\0A", align 1
