@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1779 = private unnamed_addr constant [1101 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for function docstrings that include the function's signature in\0Athe summary line.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 257] recommends against including a function's signature in its\0Adocstring. Instead, consider using type annotations as a form of\0Adocumentation for the function's parameters and return value.\0A\0AThis rule may not apply to all projects; its applicability is a matter of\0Aconvention. By default, this rule is enabled when using the `google` and\0A`pep257` conventions, and disabled when using the `numpy` convention.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef foo(a, b):\0A    \22\22\22foo(a: int, b: int) -> list[int]\22\22\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef foo(a: int, b: int) -> list[int]:\0A    \22\22\22Return a list of a and b.\22\22\22\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.pydocstyle.convention`\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 257 \E2\80\93 Docstring Conventions](https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/)\0A- [NumPy Style Guide](https://numpydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/format.html)\0A- [Google Python Style Guide - Docstrings](https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#38-comments-and-docstrings)\0A\0A[PEP 257]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/\0A", align 1
