@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1843 = private unnamed_addr constant [864 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `del` statements that delete the entire slice of a list or\0Adictionary.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIt is faster and more succinct to remove all items via the `clear()`\0Amethod.\0A\0A## Known problems\0AThis rule is prone to false negatives due to type inference limitations,\0Aas it will only detect lists and dictionaries that are instantiated as\0Aliterals or annotated with a type annotation.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Anames = {\22key\22: \22value\22}\0Anums = [1, 2, 3]\0A\0Adel names[:]\0Adel nums[:]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Anames = {\22key\22: \22value\22}\0Anums = [1, 2, 3]\0A\0Anames.clear()\0Anums.clear()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Mutable Sequence Types](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html?highlight=list#mutable-sequence-types)\0A- [Python documentation: `dict.clear()`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html?highlight=list#dict.clear)\0A", align 1
