@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1878 = private unnamed_addr constant [1684 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `Literal[True, False]` type annotations.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`Literal[True, False]` can be replaced with `bool` in type annotations,\0Awhich has the same semantic meaning but is more concise and readable.\0A\0A`bool` type has exactly two constant instances: `True` and `False`. Static\0Atype checkers such as [mypy] treat `Literal[True, False]` as equivalent to\0A`bool` in a type annotation.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom typing import Literal\0A\0Ax: Literal[True, False]\0Ay: Literal[True, False, \22hello\22, \22world\22]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom typing import Literal\0A\0Ax: bool\0Ay: Literal[\22hello\22, \22world\22] | bool\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThe fix for this rule is marked as unsafe, as it may change the semantics of the code.\0ASpecifically:\0A\0A- Type checkers may not treat `bool` as equivalent when overloading boolean arguments\0A  with `Literal[True]` and `Literal[False]` (see, e.g., [#14764] and [#5421]).\0A- `bool` is not strictly equivalent to `Literal[True, False]`, as `bool` is\0A  a subclass of `int`, and this rule may not apply if the type annotations are used\0A  in a numeric context.\0A\0AFurther, the `Literal` slice may contain trailing-line comments which the fix would remove.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Typing documentation: Legal parameters for `Literal` at type check time](https://typing.python.org/en/latest/spec/literal.html#legal-parameters-for-literal-at-type-check-time)\0A- [Python documentation: Boolean type - `bool`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#boolean-type-bool)\0A\0A[mypy]: https://github.com/python/mypy/blob/master/mypy/typeops.py#L985\0A[#14764]: https://github.com/python/mypy/issues/14764\0A[#5421]: https://github.com/microsoft/pyright/issues/5421\0A", align 1
