@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1890 = private unnamed_addr constant [49 x i8] c"unnecessary-iterable-allocation-for-first-element", align 1
@anon.faf009aaacdb3bbda30f9435f6d39aa4.1891 = private unnamed_addr constant [1472 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks the following constructs, all of which can be replaced by\0A`next(iter(...))`:\0A\0A- `list(...)[0]`\0A- `tuple(...)[0]`\0A- `list(i for i in ...)[0]`\0A- `[i for i in ...][0]`\0A- `list(...).pop(0)`\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ACalling e.g. `list(...)` will create a new list of the entire collection,\0Awhich can be very expensive for large collections. If you only need the\0Afirst element of the collection, you can use `next(...)` or\0A`next(iter(...)` to lazily fetch the first element. The same is true for\0Athe other constructs.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Ahead = list(x)[0]\0Ahead = [x * x for x in range(10)][0]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Ahead = next(iter(x))\0Ahead = next(x * x for x in range(10))\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as migrating from (e.g.) `list(...)[0]`\0Ato `next(iter(...))` can change the behavior of your program in two ways:\0A\0A1. First, all above-mentioned constructs will eagerly evaluate the entire\0A   collection, while `next(iter(...))` will only evaluate the first\0A   element. As such, any side effects that occur during iteration will be\0A   delayed.\0A2. Second, accessing members of a collection via square bracket notation\0A   `[0]` of the `pop()` function will raise `IndexError` if the collection\0A   is empty, while `next(iter(...))` will raise `StopIteration`.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Iterators and Iterables in Python: Run Efficient Iterations](https://realpython.com/python-iterators-iterables/#when-to-use-an-iterator-in-python)\0A", align 1
